		Stopped watching Artifact: "Rescue Run Sheet" (connection lost)
		Stopped watching Artifact: "Rescue Request Flow" (connection lost)
		Stopped watching Artifact: "Get-Rescue Ticket API" (connection lost)
		Look at where it's rendering: inside Ozonetel's own "Pop CRM" tab, as an embedded panel — and that grey area with the broken-document icon is the signature of a blocked iframe.
		
		Ozonetel is opening your URL. Zoho is refusing to be displayed inside it.
		
		Why
		Zoho Creator sends X-Frame-Options / frame-ancestors headers that block third-party sites from embedding app pages. Ozonetel's Pop CRM tab is a third-party iframe, so the browser drops the response and paints the empty box you're seeing.
		
		Nothing about your URL is wrong. It would load fine pasted into a normal tab.
		
		Confirm it in 10 seconds
		Open DevTools (F12) → Console on the portal page during an incoming call. You should see something like:
		
		
		Refused to display 'https://getrescued.zohocreatorportal.in/...' in a frame
		because it set 'X-Frame-Options' to 'sameorigin'.
		If that's there, it's settled.
		
		What to do — ask Ozonetel
		This is the fix, and it's a setting on their side:
		
		Our ScreenPop URL is a Zoho Creator page, which cannot be embedded in an iframe — Zoho blocks third-party framing, so your Pop CRM tab renders blank.
		
		Can ScreenPop be configured to open in a new browser tab/window instead of embedding it in the agent toolbar?
		
		Most CTI platforms offer exactly that toggle. Add it to your meeting list — it sits naturally alongside question 3.
		
		If they can only embed
		There is a route that works: Zoho's public pages (creatorapp.zohopublic.in) are designed to be embedded — that's the same URL family your customer page already uses, and it's why that one works in a WhatsApp button.
		
		So a small public Creator page hosting a cut-down screen-pop could be framed by Ozonetel legitimately. It's a new page plus a trimmed widget — maybe half a day — and I'd only build it once Ozonetel confirms they can't do new-tab, since new-tab is free and this isn't.
		
		Worth knowing either way
		Even with a new tab, the agent gets a fresh tab per call, which they'll have to close. It works, but it's not lovely.
		
		The better long-term answer is the widget asking Ozonetel about live calls itself rather than being told via a URL — which is question 1 on your meeting list ("how do we get the call ID at dial time") and the related live-call API. If Ozonetel has one, the screen-pop stops depending on URLs entirely and just appears in the page the agent already has open.
		
		
