		Those three lines inside the tag aren't iframe attributes — X-Frame-Options is a response header the target server sends, so it can't be set by the page doing the embedding, and Allow public access isn't an attribute at all. Browsers ignore all three. Let me confirm what Ozonetel actually sends.
		
		
		Confirmed — Ozonetel sends X-Frame-Options: ALLOWALL, so it already permits framing from anywhere. Nothing needs fixing on that axis; that's why the portal renders in your page while the reverse direction doesn't.
		
		The actual problem in your snippet is the sandbox attribute. It only ever subtracts permissions, never grants them, and yours is missing allow-same-origin — which puts the Ozonetel portal in an opaque origin with no access to its own cookies or localStorage. That breaks login persistence and WebRTC. Since you want full access, drop sandbox entirely:
		
		
		<%{
		%>
		<iframe
		  src="https://agent.cloudagent.ozonetel.com"
		  width="100%"
		  height="700"
		  style="border:0;display:block"
		  allow="microphone *; camera *; autoplay *; geolocation *; clipboard-write *; display-capture *"
		  allowfullscreen>
		</iframe>
		<%
		}%>
		What changed and why:
		
		Your line	Verdict
		X-Frame-Options: DENY / SAMEORIGIN	Removed — not attributes; headers come from Ozonetel's server, which already sends ALLOWALL
		Allow public access = "Yes"	Removed — not an attribute
		sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-popups"	Removed — no allow-same-origin meant no cookies/storage for the softphone
		allow="geolocation; microphone; camera"	Extended — added autoplay (ring tone won't play without it) and clipboard-write; * lets the permission survive into Ozonetel's own nested frames
		frameborder="0"	Replaced with style="border:0" — frameborder is obsolete in HTML5
		If you'd rather keep a sandbox for policy reasons, the working set is sandbox="allow-same-origin allow-scripts allow-forms allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox allow-modals allow-downloads allow-top-navigation-by-user-activation" — but note that allow-same-origin plus allow-scripts together lets the frame remove its own sandbox, so it buys you nothing over just omitting it.
		
