		That's the UCID parsing in getCallbackStatus — the stored value has a leading :" that never got stripped. Let me look at the extractor.
		
		
		Found it. The strip passes each run exactly once, in a fixed order: leading :, then leading =, then trailing }, ], then leading quote, then trailing quote. A value arriving with two junk characters at the front — : then " — gets the colon stripped, but by the time the quote pass runs the order no longer matches, so :" survives. Let me add a bounded repeat after the existing chain.
		
		
		That fixes future rows. But the 55 existing ones still hold the dirty value, and that's not just cosmetic — getCallbackStatus matches rows by Call_Log[UCID == resolvedUcid], so a row stored as :"9336… can never match a clean 9336…, and re-checking those calls would create duplicates. Here's a one-off cleaner, dry-run by default.
		
		
		Two things to do, in this order.
		
		1. Re-paste getCallbackStatus.deluge — fixes future rows.
		
		The existing strip passes each run once, in a fixed order: leading :, leading =, trailing }, trailing ], leading quote, trailing quote. A value arriving as :"9336… loses the colon on the first pass, but the quote pass has already gone by and never runs again — so the quote is stored. The order-dependence was the whole bug.
		
		The new block repeats the strip six times over both ends, so order stops mattering. It runs after the existing chain, so any value that chain already cleans starts with a digit or letter, matches nothing, and is untouched. No real value in that payload — a UCID, a timestamp, an agent name, a recording URL — legitimately begins or ends with :, =, ,, a quote or a backslash.
		
		2. Run cleanCallLogUcids.deluge once — fixes the 55 existing rows.
		
		Paste it as a standalone function and execute it with commit = no first. It writes nothing and logs every row it would change:
		
		
		Call_Log 448881000001234567: [:"93361501267909269] -> [93361501267909269]
