		What's new on Accounts Payable
		Two columns — GST Payable and GST Paid — plus a Total GST Payable line and a GST Paid input beside UTR No.
		
		The PDF's worked example reproduces: Net Fee ₹1,700 → GST Payable ₹306.
		
		Applicability comes from Vendor_GST_Number, which already existed on Vendors_Report (created 2026-08-17, unwired until now). A vendor with a GSTIN on file is a vendor who can charge GST, so I didn't add a separate yes/no flag that someone would then have to keep in step with the GSTIN itself. No GSTIN gives ₹0, not a blank — the column always reconciles.
		
		GST Paid is typed, not calculated, per the PDF's "Accounts" source. Left blank it records the full GST Payable, which is the normal case. It's deliberately not part of the Record Payment readiness check — a vendor with no GSTIN has nothing to enter there, and requiring it would block their payment.
		
		18% isn't per-vendor, unlike TDS. GST is set by the service category, not negotiated per supplier.
		
		One refactor, with a regression test
		GST Paid needed the same batch split as TDS, so I extracted allocateProportional() and pointed allocateBatchTds() at it. A second copy of largest-remainder logic is exactly how two money columns end up disagreeing by a paisa with nobody able to say which is right.
		
		I tested that TDS is unchanged by the refactor — three uneven fees at 1% still give [17, 12.33, 9.91] summing to 39.24. The allocator also holds up on the awkward cases: [306,306,306] over ₹100 gives [33.34, 33.33, 33.33] summing to exactly 100, and zero weights produce zeros rather than inventing a split.
		
